{"id":"7f07f6b984f4433f8241cd31ad412663","model":"gpt-oss-120b","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"answer\": \"noanswer\",\n  \"citations\": []\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to answer: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" Need to interpret: Which UK Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party? Likely refers to a PM who was succeeded (i.e., replaced) by someone who was the Leader of the Conservative Party. In the 1990s, there were several PM changes: Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major in 1990 (Major was Leader of Conservative Party). Also, John Major was succeeded by Tony Blair (Labour) in 1997, but Blair was not Conservative leader. Also, James Callaghan (Labour) succeeded by Margaret Thatcher (Conservative) in 1979, not 1990s. So the question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" That suggests a PM who left office in the 1990s, and the successor was the Leader of the Conservative Party. In 1990, Margaret Thatcher (Conservative PM) was succeeded by John Major, who was Leader of the Conservative Party. So the answer: Margaret Thatcher.\n\nWe need evidence: The evidence includes a sentence: \"These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.\" That's about 1980. Not directly about succession. We need evidence that Thatcher was succeeded by John Major in 1990. Not present in given evidence. Let's search within evidence: There's mention of \"The 1997 Conservative Party leadership election was triggered ... John Major resigned on 2 May 1997, following his party's defeat at the 1997 general election, which ended 18 years of Conservative Government\". That indicates Major was leader after Thatcher. But we need evidence that Thatcher was succeeded by Major. Not present. There's also \"The Federation